Solo task locks: acquisition ownership
=====================================

acquire_solo_lock(name, ttl_seconds) returns the acquisition's opaque token, or
None while the key is occupied. A caller retains that token and passes it to
release_solo_lock(name, token) in its finally block. Redis compares the exact
token and deletes only that acquisition. A process-global dictionary is no
longer used to find a token by task name.

This matters when a slow run outlives its lease and another run acquires the
same name in the same worker/API process. The old run cannot release the new
run's token, even if completion order is reversed. Repeated releases and a
token for a different key also leave the current owner's lease intact.

All 15 release sites are migrated: discovery, metrics, hypervisor/storage/
firewall/Omada/overlay polling, status synchronization, reconciliation and
health aggregation, gateway synchronization, and event replay. The existing
on-demand reconciliation cooldown still intentionally leaves its key to expire;
it uses acquisition truthiness and does not need a release token.

Custom extensions using these internal Python helpers must retain the returned
token and pass it explicitly on release. Old one-argument release calls are no
longer accepted. Deploy the complete API/worker revision together; replacing
only the helper while retaining old caller code is unsupported.

Acquisition failures propagate before work starts. Release transport failures
are logged without exception details and do not mask a task result or trigger
a repeat of effects that have already completed. Existing lease expiry still
provides abandoned-lock cleanup. Invalid/missing tokens are programmer errors.

Limits
------
A finite Redis lease does not prevent overlap after expiration, pause, a network
partition, or lost Redis state. There is no renewal thread, durable fencing
token checked by devices/databases, exactly-once execution, or transaction
around a remote side effect. This change prevents an old release from deleting
a newer lease. It does not make a stale worker stop work after losing ownership.
The lock key continues to carry the caller's existing scope; no authorization or
tenant boundaries are broadened by this helper change.

Qualification distinguishes deterministic unit checks, private Redis checks,
and the shipping Valkey image. Real-server checks force expiration of owned
fixture keys and reacquire them in the same process, including through all 15
actual wrappers. Device and database work is synthetic; no hardware action or
external notification is sent. Atomic contention remains a separate control.
